1
00:00:10,003 --> 00:00:11,233
In the year 79,

2
00:00:11,233 --> 00:00:13,954
a Roman city was living a completely ordinary

3
00:00:13,954 --> 00:00:14,773
day

4
00:00:14,923 --> 00:00:15,953
The market was crowded,

5
00:00:15,953 --> 00:00:17,433
the bakery ovens glowed,

6
00:00:17,433 --> 00:00:19,914
and fresh election slogans were painted on the

7
00:00:19,914 --> 00:00:20,969
walls

8
00:00:21,119 --> 00:00:22,430
Less than two days later,

9
00:00:22,430 --> 00:00:24,671
it had all vanished under metres of ash and

10
00:00:24,671 --> 00:00:25,151
stone,

11
00:00:25,151 --> 00:00:27,752
and it was forgotten for nearly 1,700 years

12
00:00:29,337 --> 00:00:30,888
This is the story of Pompeii,

13
00:00:30,888 --> 00:00:32,968
told by stick figures

14
00:00:39,845 --> 00:00:41,596
Pompeii lay on the Bay of Naples,

15
00:00:41,596 --> 00:00:42,596
in southern Italy,

16
00:00:42,596 --> 00:00:44,797
less than ten kilometres from the summit of

17
00:00:44,797 --> 00:00:46,237
Mount Vesuvius

18
00:00:46,965 --> 00:00:48,356
It was a busy port town,

19
00:00:48,356 --> 00:00:50,957
home to more than ten thousand people

20
00:00:51,518 --> 00:00:53,228
Its people grew grapes for wine,

21
00:00:53,228 --> 00:00:55,709
made a fermented fish sauce called garum,

22
00:00:55,709 --> 00:00:58,310
and shipped it all across the Mediterranean

23
00:00:59,149 --> 00:01:01,340
The town had around eighty hot-food counters,

24
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:03,941
a lot like today's fast-food places

25
00:01:04,996 --> 00:01:05,906
There were public baths,

26
00:01:05,906 --> 00:01:06,246
theatres,

27
00:01:06,246 --> 00:01:08,507
and a stone amphitheatre that could hold about

28
00:01:08,507 --> 00:01:10,268
twenty thousand people

29
00:01:11,216 --> 00:01:13,207
The walls all over town were covered in

30
00:01:13,207 --> 00:01:13,647
writing:

31
00:01:13,647 --> 00:01:14,340
adverts,

32
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:15,727
election slogans,

33
00:01:15,727 --> 00:01:16,488
love notes,

34
00:01:16,488 --> 00:01:17,928
even insults

35
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:19,597
One person wrote:

36
00:01:19,597 --> 00:01:20,291
O wall,

37
00:01:20,291 --> 00:01:22,291
I am amazed you have not collapsed,

38
00:01:22,291 --> 00:01:24,492
carrying so much rubbish scribbled all over

39
00:01:24,492 --> 00:01:25,612
you

40
00:01:26,708 --> 00:01:28,578
And the mountain behind the town was covered in

41
00:01:28,578 --> 00:01:30,019
green vineyards

42
00:01:30,599 --> 00:01:32,870
Almost nobody knew that it was a sleeping

43
00:01:32,870 --> 00:01:33,990
volcano

44
00:01:40,798 --> 00:01:42,028
On the fifth of February,

45
00:01:42,028 --> 00:01:43,469
in the year 62,

46
00:01:43,469 --> 00:01:45,870
a powerful earthquake shook the whole Campania

47
00:01:45,870 --> 00:01:46,990
region

48
00:01:47,853 --> 00:01:48,683
Temples cracked,

49
00:01:48,683 --> 00:01:50,244
bronze statues broke,

50
00:01:50,244 --> 00:01:52,845
and many houses in Pompeii collapsed

51
00:01:53,684 --> 00:01:55,715
The philosopher Seneca recorded something

52
00:01:55,715 --> 00:01:56,235
strange:

53
00:01:56,235 --> 00:01:59,036
a flock of six hundred sheep died mysteriously

54
00:01:59,036 --> 00:02:00,796
after the earthquake

55
00:02:01,798 --> 00:02:03,509
The people of Pompeii did not leave

56
00:02:03,509 --> 00:02:04,309
They cleared up,

57
00:02:04,309 --> 00:02:05,750
and rebuilt

58
00:02:06,224 --> 00:02:07,374
Seventeen years later,

59
00:02:07,374 --> 00:02:09,415
many buildings were still under repair

60
00:02:09,415 --> 00:02:11,455
Workers were still plastering walls and

61
00:02:11,455 --> 00:02:12,896
repainting frescoes

62
00:02:14,501 --> 00:02:16,252
The ground still trembled now and then

63
00:02:16,252 --> 00:02:17,292
In the final days,

64
00:02:17,292 --> 00:02:19,693
the tremors came more often

65
00:02:20,076 --> 00:02:20,786
But in this region,

66
00:02:20,786 --> 00:02:21,987
earthquakes were normal

67
00:02:21,987 --> 00:02:23,747
Nobody was worried

68
00:02:24,339 --> 00:02:25,890
They did not know that deep below,

69
00:02:25,890 --> 00:02:28,491
a huge mass of molten rock was rising

70
00:02:29,571 --> 00:02:30,001
Today,

71
00:02:30,001 --> 00:02:32,882
many scientists think the earthquake of 62 may

72
00:02:32,882 --> 00:02:35,483
have been the first warning from Vesuvius

73
00:02:42,217 --> 00:02:43,247
The year 79

74
00:02:43,247 --> 00:02:44,127
Across the bay,

75
00:02:44,127 --> 00:02:46,008
at the naval base of Misenum,

76
00:02:46,008 --> 00:02:48,289
lived a seventeen year old boy named Pliny

77
00:02:48,289 --> 00:02:50,890
History calls him Pliny the Younger

78
00:02:51,827 --> 00:02:52,817
Early that afternoon,

79
00:02:52,817 --> 00:02:54,818
his mother called his uncle to look at a

80
00:02:54,818 --> 00:02:56,018
very strange cloud,

81
00:02:56,018 --> 00:02:58,619
rising from the direction of the mountain

82
00:02:59,498 --> 00:03:01,649
Pliny said it looked like a giant pine tree:

83
00:03:01,649 --> 00:03:04,050
a tall trunk shooting straight up into the sky,

84
00:03:04,050 --> 00:03:06,450
then spreading out high above

85
00:03:07,404 --> 00:03:09,114
It was a column of ash and pumice,

86
00:03:09,114 --> 00:03:11,355
blasted more than thirty kilometres high,

87
00:03:11,355 --> 00:03:13,956
nearly four times the height of Everest

88
00:03:15,321 --> 00:03:17,402
Vesuvius had woken up

89
00:03:19,265 --> 00:03:20,816
About twenty five years later,

90
00:03:20,816 --> 00:03:23,257
Pliny described it all in two letters to the

91
00:03:23,257 --> 00:03:24,697
historian Tacitus

92
00:03:26,030 --> 00:03:27,581
They are the only eyewitness account that

93
00:03:27,581 --> 00:03:28,381
survives today

94
00:03:28,381 --> 00:03:30,702
This kind of eruption was later named after

95
00:03:30,702 --> 00:03:30,902
him:

96
00:03:30,902 --> 00:03:32,662
a Plinian eruption

97
00:03:33,948 --> 00:03:35,138
According to the letters,

98
00:03:35,138 --> 00:03:37,099
that day was the 24th of August

99
00:03:37,099 --> 00:03:38,859
Remember that date

100
00:03:45,302 --> 00:03:46,933
The wind was blowing to the southeast,

101
00:03:46,933 --> 00:03:49,357
pushing the whole ash cloud straight down onto

102
00:03:49,357 --> 00:03:50,477
Pompeii

103
00:03:51,289 --> 00:03:52,960
The sky went dark in the middle of the

104
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:53,480
afternoon

105
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:54,440
From high above,

106
00:03:54,440 --> 00:03:55,841
white and grey pumice,

107
00:03:55,841 --> 00:03:57,241
light and full of holes,

108
00:03:57,241 --> 00:03:59,002
fell like hail

109
00:03:59,715 --> 00:04:02,025
A single piece of pumice is light enough to

110
00:04:02,025 --> 00:04:02,866
float on water

111
00:04:02,866 --> 00:04:03,586
But piled up,

112
00:04:03,586 --> 00:04:06,187
it is as heavy as real stone

113
00:04:06,875 --> 00:04:07,465
Every hour,

114
00:04:07,465 --> 00:04:09,626
the pumice grew about fifteen centimetres

115
00:04:09,626 --> 00:04:09,986
deeper

116
00:04:09,986 --> 00:04:11,746
People had to light lamps in the middle of

117
00:04:11,746 --> 00:04:13,187
the day

118
00:04:13,721 --> 00:04:14,951
The people had to choose:

119
00:04:14,951 --> 00:04:15,352
run,

120
00:04:15,352 --> 00:04:16,792
or stay?

121
00:04:18,465 --> 00:04:19,335
Most of them ran,

122
00:04:19,335 --> 00:04:21,055
taking whatever they could carry:

123
00:04:21,055 --> 00:04:21,555
money,

124
00:04:21,555 --> 00:04:22,056
jewellery,

125
00:04:22,056 --> 00:04:23,456
and even their house keys,

126
00:04:23,456 --> 00:04:26,057
as if they would soon be back

127
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:28,451
Others shut their doors and hid inside,

128
00:04:28,451 --> 00:04:31,051
waiting for the stone rain to pass

129
00:04:32,122 --> 00:04:33,192
But it did not pass

130
00:04:33,192 --> 00:04:34,672
For about seventeen hours,

131
00:04:34,672 --> 00:04:36,113
the pumice kept falling,

132
00:04:36,113 --> 00:04:38,514
piling up on the roofs

133
00:04:39,061 --> 00:04:41,091
The layer grew almost three metres deep

134
00:04:41,091 --> 00:04:41,892
One by one,

135
00:04:41,892 --> 00:04:44,493
roofs began to collapse under the weight

136
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:46,950
And the pumice was not yet the worst of

137
00:04:46,950 --> 00:04:48,071
it

138
00:04:54,658 --> 00:04:56,968
Pliny the Younger's uncle was Pliny the Elder,

139
00:04:56,968 --> 00:04:59,449
admiral of the Roman fleet at Misenum,

140
00:04:59,449 --> 00:05:01,530
and a famous scientist

141
00:05:02,398 --> 00:05:04,349
He wanted to sail closer for a better look

142
00:05:04,349 --> 00:05:05,869
He invited his nephew along,

143
00:05:05,869 --> 00:05:08,470
but the boy asked to stay home and study

144
00:05:09,897 --> 00:05:11,408
Just as he stepped out of the door,

145
00:05:11,408 --> 00:05:13,608
a plea for help arrived from Rectina,

146
00:05:13,608 --> 00:05:15,369
whose villa stood right at the foot of the

147
00:05:15,369 --> 00:05:15,769
mountain

148
00:05:15,769 --> 00:05:18,370
The only way out was by sea

149
00:05:19,055 --> 00:05:21,126
The science trip instantly became a rescue

150
00:05:21,126 --> 00:05:21,406
mission

151
00:05:21,406 --> 00:05:23,326
He launched the big warships and headed

152
00:05:23,326 --> 00:05:25,927
straight for the people trying to flee

153
00:05:26,621 --> 00:05:28,732
Hot ash and pumice rained onto the decks

154
00:05:28,732 --> 00:05:29,572
Near the shore,

155
00:05:29,572 --> 00:05:31,213
floating stone blocked the way in

156
00:05:31,213 --> 00:05:33,814
The helmsman urged him to turn back

157
00:05:34,629 --> 00:05:35,299
He replied:

158
00:05:35,299 --> 00:05:36,700
Fortune favours the brave

159
00:05:36,700 --> 00:05:38,460
Head for Stabiae!

160
00:05:39,901 --> 00:05:40,891
At Stabiae,

161
00:05:40,891 --> 00:05:42,011
he still bathed,

162
00:05:42,011 --> 00:05:42,651
ate dinner,

163
00:05:42,651 --> 00:05:43,652
and went to bed,

164
00:05:43,652 --> 00:05:46,253
to calm the panicking people around him

165
00:05:47,551 --> 00:05:48,461
At dawn the next day,

166
00:05:48,461 --> 00:05:49,742
the sky was still black

167
00:05:49,742 --> 00:05:51,382
He went down to the beach to find a

168
00:05:51,382 --> 00:05:51,782
way out,

169
00:05:51,782 --> 00:05:54,383
but the waves were still too rough

170
00:05:55,071 --> 00:05:56,302
He collapsed on the sand,

171
00:05:56,302 --> 00:05:57,582
and never got up again

172
00:05:57,582 --> 00:05:58,742
When daylight returned,

173
00:05:58,742 --> 00:06:00,023
they found him lying there,

174
00:06:00,023 --> 00:06:02,624
looking as if he were asleep

175
00:06:09,347 --> 00:06:11,177
West of the mountain lay Herculaneum,

176
00:06:11,177 --> 00:06:13,938
a wealthy seaside town of about five thousand

177
00:06:13,938 --> 00:06:15,058
people

178
00:06:15,903 --> 00:06:17,933
The wind was carrying the ash the other way,

179
00:06:17,933 --> 00:06:19,054
so all afternoon,

180
00:06:19,054 --> 00:06:21,454
almost no stones fell here

181
00:06:22,145 --> 00:06:23,695
Many people managed to escape

182
00:06:23,695 --> 00:06:25,656
Hundreds of others crowded onto the beach,

183
00:06:25,656 --> 00:06:28,096
squeezing into the stone boat sheds to wait for

184
00:06:28,096 --> 00:06:29,537
rescue ships

185
00:06:30,501 --> 00:06:30,971
That night,

186
00:06:30,971 --> 00:06:33,572
the giant ash column began to collapse

187
00:06:35,318 --> 00:06:36,428
A cloud of hot gas,

188
00:06:36,428 --> 00:06:37,508
ash and rock,

189
00:06:37,508 --> 00:06:38,989
hundreds of degrees hot,

190
00:06:38,989 --> 00:06:41,429
raced down the mountainside faster than a car

191
00:06:41,429 --> 00:06:43,190
on a motorway

192
00:06:43,836 --> 00:06:46,107
Scientists call it a pyroclastic flow

193
00:06:46,107 --> 00:06:48,508
No one could outrun it

194
00:06:49,399 --> 00:06:52,230
In the 1980s archaeologists dug down to the

195
00:06:52,230 --> 00:06:52,950
ancient beach,

196
00:06:52,950 --> 00:06:54,671
and found the remains of more than three

197
00:06:54,671 --> 00:06:57,271
hundred people inside the boat sheds

198
00:06:58,489 --> 00:06:58,999
Nearby,

199
00:06:58,999 --> 00:07:00,919
they also found a unique case:

200
00:07:00,919 --> 00:07:03,120
the brain tissue of a young man had turned

201
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:03,880
into glass,

202
00:07:03,880 --> 00:07:06,041
heated above five hundred degrees and then

203
00:07:06,041 --> 00:07:07,802
cooled extremely fast

204
00:07:09,251 --> 00:07:11,842
Herculaneum was buried more than twenty metres

205
00:07:11,842 --> 00:07:12,962
deep

206
00:07:19,402 --> 00:07:20,953
At dawn on the second day,

207
00:07:20,953 --> 00:07:23,353
Pompeii was still in darkness

208
00:07:24,463 --> 00:07:26,253
Those who had stayed began to look for a

209
00:07:26,253 --> 00:07:26,853
way out,

210
00:07:26,853 --> 00:07:29,214
walking on top of pumice nearly three metres

211
00:07:29,214 --> 00:07:30,335
deep

212
00:07:31,088 --> 00:07:32,678
Just after seven in the morning,

213
00:07:32,678 --> 00:07:35,279
a pyroclastic flow swept over the city walls

214
00:07:37,392 --> 00:07:38,062
Within minutes,

215
00:07:38,062 --> 00:07:39,983
the whole city was buried in hot ash

216
00:07:39,983 --> 00:07:42,223
About half of the victims in Pompeii were found

217
00:07:42,223 --> 00:07:43,264
in this very layer,

218
00:07:43,264 --> 00:07:45,864
right in the middle of the streets

219
00:07:46,787 --> 00:07:47,577
Across the bay,

220
00:07:47,577 --> 00:07:49,697
Pliny the Younger and his mother also had to

221
00:07:49,697 --> 00:07:52,098
flee through a panicking crowd

222
00:07:52,691 --> 00:07:53,042
He wrote:

223
00:07:53,042 --> 00:07:55,122
the darkness was not like a moonless night,

224
00:07:55,122 --> 00:07:57,123
but like a sealed room when the lamp has

225
00:07:57,123 --> 00:07:58,883
been put out

226
00:07:59,964 --> 00:08:00,834
Women were crying,

227
00:08:00,834 --> 00:08:02,034
children screaming,

228
00:08:02,034 --> 00:08:03,955
men calling out each other's names

229
00:08:03,955 --> 00:08:05,595
Many believed the gods were gone,

230
00:08:05,595 --> 00:08:07,276
and that this was the last night of the

231
00:08:07,276 --> 00:08:08,396
world

232
00:08:09,380 --> 00:08:10,770
Mother and son survived

233
00:08:10,770 --> 00:08:12,450
By the evening of the second day,

234
00:08:12,450 --> 00:08:13,891
after about thirty two hours,

235
00:08:13,891 --> 00:08:16,292
the volcano finally fell silent

236
00:08:17,506 --> 00:08:17,949
Pompeii,

237
00:08:17,949 --> 00:08:18,442
Herculaneum,

238
00:08:18,442 --> 00:08:19,923
Stabiae,

239
00:08:19,923 --> 00:08:20,417
Oplontis

240
00:08:20,417 --> 00:08:22,818
All of them were gone

241
00:08:28,689 --> 00:08:29,000
Emperor Titus sent help,

242
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,001
using the property of those who had died

243
00:08:32,001 --> 00:08:34,601
without heirs to support the survivors

244
00:08:35,490 --> 00:08:36,880
But Pompeii was never rebuilt

245
00:08:36,880 --> 00:08:38,400
The survivors scattered to Naples,

246
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:40,161
Cumae and Puteoli

247
00:08:41,696 --> 00:08:42,847
In 2025,

248
00:08:42,847 --> 00:08:45,448
archaeologists also discovered that some poor

249
00:08:45,448 --> 00:08:46,448
people had come back,

250
00:08:46,448 --> 00:08:48,729
camping on the upper floors of houses that

251
00:08:48,729 --> 00:08:51,329
still stuck out of the ash

252
00:08:52,065 --> 00:08:53,175
By the fifth century,

253
00:08:53,175 --> 00:08:54,976
the place was completely abandoned

254
00:08:54,976 --> 00:08:56,056
Grass grew over it

255
00:08:56,056 --> 00:08:58,657
Even the name Pompeii was forgotten

256
00:08:59,715 --> 00:09:02,066
Locals simply called the hill La Civita,

257
00:09:02,066 --> 00:09:03,826
meaning the city

258
00:09:04,706 --> 00:09:06,176
In the late sixteenth century,

259
00:09:06,176 --> 00:09:08,417
a canal was dug straight through Pompeii

260
00:09:08,417 --> 00:09:10,297
Walls and inscriptions turned up,

261
00:09:10,297 --> 00:09:12,378
but nobody paid attention

262
00:09:13,552 --> 00:09:14,863
In 1631,

263
00:09:14,863 --> 00:09:16,623
Vesuvius erupted again,

264
00:09:16,623 --> 00:09:18,704
killing thousands of people

265
00:09:19,948 --> 00:09:21,259
In 1738,

266
00:09:21,259 --> 00:09:23,659
the King of Naples had Herculaneum dug up to

267
00:09:23,659 --> 00:09:24,940
hunt for ancient statues

268
00:09:24,940 --> 00:09:26,300
In 1748,

269
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:28,381
it was Pompeii's turn

270
00:09:29,259 --> 00:09:30,690
In 1763,

271
00:09:30,690 --> 00:09:33,010
a stone inscription was found that clearly

272
00:09:33,010 --> 00:09:34,451
named Pompeii

273
00:09:41,412 --> 00:09:42,602
For more than a century,

274
00:09:42,602 --> 00:09:45,323
Pompeii was dug mainly to hunt for statues and

275
00:09:45,323 --> 00:09:46,764
valuable paintings

276
00:09:48,084 --> 00:09:49,514
In 1863,

277
00:09:49,514 --> 00:09:52,155
the archaeologist Giuseppe Fiorelli noticed

278
00:09:52,155 --> 00:09:52,995
something strange:

279
00:09:52,995 --> 00:09:54,036
in the hardened ash,

280
00:09:54,036 --> 00:09:56,116
there were hollow spaces

281
00:09:57,116 --> 00:09:59,040
The hot ash had sealed around the victims

282
00:09:59,040 --> 00:09:59,787
Over time,

283
00:09:59,787 --> 00:10:00,907
the bodies decayed,

284
00:10:00,907 --> 00:10:03,508
leaving an empty mould in exactly their shape

285
00:10:04,778 --> 00:10:07,409
Fiorelli poured liquid plaster into the moulds,

286
00:10:07,409 --> 00:10:08,529
waited for it to set,

287
00:10:08,529 --> 00:10:10,930
then carefully removed the ash

288
00:10:12,254 --> 00:10:13,845
What appeared were people in their final

289
00:10:13,845 --> 00:10:14,325
moments

290
00:10:14,325 --> 00:10:15,245
Some curled up,

291
00:10:15,245 --> 00:10:16,606
some covering their faces,

292
00:10:16,606 --> 00:10:19,006
some lying side by side

293
00:10:19,928 --> 00:10:20,918
There was even a dog,

294
00:10:20,918 --> 00:10:22,238
still wearing its collar,

295
00:10:22,238 --> 00:10:24,839
chained in front of a house

296
00:10:25,582 --> 00:10:27,293
Today there are more than a hundred plaster

297
00:10:27,293 --> 00:10:28,253
casts like these

298
00:10:28,253 --> 00:10:30,534
The same method was used to recast wooden

299
00:10:30,534 --> 00:10:31,014
doors,

300
00:10:31,014 --> 00:10:31,614
furniture,

301
00:10:31,614 --> 00:10:34,215
and even tree roots in gardens

302
00:10:35,019 --> 00:10:37,850
Fiorelli also divided the city into regions and

303
00:10:37,850 --> 00:10:38,290
blocks,

304
00:10:38,290 --> 00:10:39,611
and numbered every doorway

305
00:10:39,611 --> 00:10:42,212
That system is still used today

306
00:10:43,053 --> 00:10:43,903
From then on,

307
00:10:43,903 --> 00:10:45,804
Pompeii was no longer a treasure trove

308
00:10:45,804 --> 00:10:48,084
It became a window into the everyday life of

309
00:10:48,084 --> 00:10:49,525
the Romans

310
00:10:56,202 --> 00:10:57,952
Remember the 24th of August,

311
00:10:57,952 --> 00:10:59,713
from Pliny's letter?

312
00:11:00,751 --> 00:11:01,822
In 2018,

313
00:11:01,822 --> 00:11:04,662
archaeologists found a line written in charcoal

314
00:11:04,662 --> 00:11:05,783
on the wall of a house,

315
00:11:05,783 --> 00:11:08,384
with a date equal to the 17th of October

316
00:11:10,236 --> 00:11:11,907
Charcoal fades very easily

317
00:11:11,907 --> 00:11:14,107
It was most likely written just days before the

318
00:11:14,107 --> 00:11:15,228
disaster

319
00:11:16,470 --> 00:11:17,901
Add to that pomegranates,

320
00:11:17,901 --> 00:11:19,741
figs and walnuts from autumn,

321
00:11:19,741 --> 00:11:21,862
charcoal braziers inside homes,

322
00:11:21,862 --> 00:11:24,463
and new wine sealed in jars

323
00:11:25,395 --> 00:11:27,345
Many researchers now believe the eruption

324
00:11:27,345 --> 00:11:28,866
happened in late October,

325
00:11:28,866 --> 00:11:29,586
not August

326
00:11:29,586 --> 00:11:32,187
But the debate is not over

327
00:11:33,117 --> 00:11:34,148
In 2024,

328
00:11:34,148 --> 00:11:37,349
scientists extracted DNA from bones inside some

329
00:11:37,349 --> 00:11:39,429
of the plaster casts

330
00:11:40,323 --> 00:11:42,514
An adult wearing a gold bracelet and holding a

331
00:11:42,514 --> 00:11:42,914
child,

332
00:11:42,914 --> 00:11:45,075
long described as a mother and her baby,

333
00:11:45,075 --> 00:11:46,435
turned out to be a man,

334
00:11:46,435 --> 00:11:48,836
not related to the child

335
00:11:49,730 --> 00:11:50,881
Two people embracing,

336
00:11:50,881 --> 00:11:52,361
once said to be sisters,

337
00:11:52,361 --> 00:11:54,762
included at least one man

338
00:11:55,978 --> 00:11:58,489
The DNA also showed that many people in Pompeii

339
00:11:58,489 --> 00:12:00,570
had ancestors from the eastern Mediterranean

340
00:12:00,570 --> 00:12:03,170
The city was far more diverse than we thought

341
00:12:09,858 --> 00:12:11,968
In Herculaneum stood a seaside villa that may

342
00:12:11,968 --> 00:12:14,569
have belonged to Julius Caesar's father-in-law

343
00:12:15,751 --> 00:12:17,182
In the mid eighteenth century,

344
00:12:17,182 --> 00:12:20,463
about 1,800 papyrus scrolls and fragments were

345
00:12:20,463 --> 00:12:20,983
found there,

346
00:12:20,983 --> 00:12:23,064
burnt black like charcoal

347
00:12:24,038 --> 00:12:25,908
It is the only library from the ancient world

348
00:12:25,908 --> 00:12:26,789
that survives today

349
00:12:26,789 --> 00:12:28,509
But just touching the scrolls makes them

350
00:12:28,509 --> 00:12:29,630
crumble

351
00:12:30,936 --> 00:12:32,046
In 2023,

352
00:12:32,046 --> 00:12:34,287
the Vesuvius Challenge scanned the scrolls

353
00:12:34,287 --> 00:12:35,287
layer by layer,

354
00:12:35,287 --> 00:12:37,448
then invited the whole world to read them with

355
00:12:37,448 --> 00:12:38,888
artificial intelligence

356
00:12:40,418 --> 00:12:41,088
That October,

357
00:12:41,088 --> 00:12:43,329
a twenty one year old student read the very

358
00:12:43,329 --> 00:12:45,770
first word inside a scroll that had never been

359
00:12:45,770 --> 00:12:46,210
opened:

360
00:12:46,210 --> 00:12:47,330
purple

361
00:12:48,829 --> 00:12:50,139
In early 2024,

362
00:12:50,139 --> 00:12:52,980
three young people won a $700,000 prize for

363
00:12:52,980 --> 00:12:55,100
reading more than two thousand characters about

364
00:12:55,100 --> 00:12:56,221
pleasure

365
00:12:57,338 --> 00:12:58,408
In 2025,

366
00:12:58,408 --> 00:12:59,369
for the first time,

367
00:12:59,369 --> 00:13:00,769
a book title was read:

368
00:13:00,769 --> 00:13:01,489
On Vices,

369
00:13:01,489 --> 00:13:03,570
by the philosopher Philodemus

370
00:13:05,514 --> 00:13:08,665
By mid-2026 more than seventy columns of text

371
00:13:08,665 --> 00:13:11,265
had been read from that scroll alone

372
00:13:12,258 --> 00:13:13,928
Hundreds of scrolls remain unread

373
00:13:13,928 --> 00:13:15,889
Some of them may hold works lost for two

374
00:13:15,889 --> 00:13:17,329
thousand years

375
00:13:24,147 --> 00:13:26,778
On the night of 24 August 1943,

376
00:13:26,778 --> 00:13:28,899
the very date history books gave for the

377
00:13:28,899 --> 00:13:30,179
destruction of Pompeii,

378
00:13:30,179 --> 00:13:32,580
Allied planes bombed the ruins

379
00:13:33,595 --> 00:13:34,466
Within two months,

380
00:13:34,466 --> 00:13:36,906
more than one hundred and sixty bombs hit the

381
00:13:36,906 --> 00:13:38,347
ancient city

382
00:13:39,234 --> 00:13:40,704
In March 1944,

383
00:13:40,704 --> 00:13:43,105
Vesuvius erupted for the most recent time

384
00:13:43,105 --> 00:13:45,346
Hot ash and rock fell on an American air

385
00:13:45,346 --> 00:13:46,786
base at the foot of the mountain,

386
00:13:46,786 --> 00:13:48,867
destroying about eighty bombers

387
00:13:50,086 --> 00:13:50,597
Since then,

388
00:13:50,597 --> 00:13:52,077
the volcano has been quiet

389
00:13:52,077 --> 00:13:54,478
But it is not extinct

390
00:13:55,182 --> 00:13:55,612
Today,

391
00:13:55,612 --> 00:13:58,573
about 700,000 people live in the red zone

392
00:13:58,573 --> 00:13:59,573
around Vesuvius

393
00:13:59,573 --> 00:14:00,934
If an alert is raised,

394
00:14:00,934 --> 00:14:03,534
they must all be evacuated within three days

395
00:14:05,106 --> 00:14:05,856
Not far away,

396
00:14:05,856 --> 00:14:08,537
the ground of the Campi Flegrei volcanic area

397
00:14:08,537 --> 00:14:09,457
is slowly rising

398
00:14:09,457 --> 00:14:11,018
In July 2026,

399
00:14:11,018 --> 00:14:13,459
a magnitude 4.7 earthquake,

400
00:14:13,459 --> 00:14:15,199
the strongest in forty years,

401
00:14:15,199 --> 00:14:16,640
shook Naples

402
00:14:17,889 --> 00:14:18,639
As for Pompeii,

403
00:14:18,639 --> 00:14:21,160
it now welcomes more than four million visitors

404
00:14:21,160 --> 00:14:21,680
a year,

405
00:14:21,680 --> 00:14:23,801
so many that numbers are capped at twenty

406
00:14:23,801 --> 00:14:25,561
thousand a day

407
00:14:26,163 --> 00:14:28,234
And nearly a third of the city still lies

408
00:14:28,234 --> 00:14:28,994
under the ash,

409
00:14:28,994 --> 00:14:31,595
waiting for its turn to tell its story

410
00:14:34,669 --> 00:14:36,780
Pompeii is not just the story of a disaster

411
00:14:36,780 --> 00:14:38,660
It is an ordinary day of human life,

412
00:14:38,660 --> 00:14:41,261
preserved for nearly two thousand years

413
00:14:41,705 --> 00:14:43,496
If you had lived in Pompeii that day,

414
00:14:43,496 --> 00:14:45,216
when the pumice began to fall,

415
00:14:45,216 --> 00:14:45,977
would you run,

416
00:14:45,977 --> 00:14:46,937
or would you stay?

417
00:14:46,937 --> 00:14:49,338
Tell me in the comments
